 - **Metadata, defined once.** Each config field carries Zod registry metadata in `packages/contracts` (`.meta(field('…', { advanced, context, group, title, control }))`; `meta.ts`): its description, whether it is advanced, the heading it is grouped under, a label when the humanized key would not say it (`title`), and the name of a custom control. `fieldMeta(schema)` in contracts reads it (merged along optional, default, and prefault wrappers); the forms and the generated node reference (`pnpm docs:generate`, which marks advanced fields in an Advanced column) both use it, and `z.toJSONSchema` emits it. `.meta()` copies a schema with the same definition, so parsing is unchanged; code that recognises a shared schema by identity (`TemplateSchema`, `ExpressionSchema`, `JsonSchemaSchema`: the form's introspection and the domain's template and expression checks) compares with `sameSchema`, which sees through the copy.
 - **Placement.** `forms/layout.ts` applies the same metadata-driven layout to top-level and nested object/union bodies. An always-present object splits its tagged children between basic and Advanced; optional objects and collections retain their existing Add/Remove lifecycle. Required fields override an Advanced tag. `contextLayout` walks present objects and the selected evaluator variant to collect tagged Context fields without rewriting their paths or values, applying the form's top-level `fieldOrder` as Settings does. Those fields are omitted from Settings and rendered once on Context. No contract shape, parse output, migration or stored-data change is involved.
-- **Context tabs (#170).** `components/ui/tabs.tsx` supplies horizontal, automatically activated Settings/Context tabs: one tab stop, Left/Right with wraparound, Home/End, `aria-selected`, and linked `aria-controls`/`aria-labelledby` panels. Tabs count problems, Context also counts fields with their own values, and Settings has no indicator for valid required controls because every dialog opening starts on Settings. Each problem badge counts each issue path once. Context also shows how many placed context fields hold a value of their own ("2 set"), using the Advanced counting rules below, in both its text and accessible name. A provider Context object counts once when any child is customized; the inference prompt, a non-default session, input and context files count separately. Both panels remain mounted inside one form so values, validation and held unparsed text survive switching. `#context-tab` in the dialog disclosure session stores selection; reopening starts on Settings, while undo/redo remounts preserve the user's tab. Advanced states are separate for Settings, Context and nested object bodies. Node id/label and Connections stay outside the config tabs. The primitive decision is [ADR-0031](decisions/ADR-0031-mounted-context-tabs.md).
+- **Context tabs (#170).** `components/ui/tabs.tsx` supplies horizontal, automatically activated Settings/Context tabs: one tab stop, Left/Right with wraparound, Home/End, `aria-selected`, and linked `aria-controls`/`aria-labelledby` panels. Tabs count problems, Context also counts fields with their own values, and Settings has no indicator for valid required controls because every dialog opening starts on Settings. Each problem badge counts each issue path once. Context also shows how many placed context fields hold a value of their own ("2 set"), using the Advanced counting rules below, in both its text and accessible name. A provider Context object counts once when any child is customized; the inference prompt, a non-default session, input and context files count separately. Both panels remain mounted inside one form so values, validation and held unparsed text survive switching. `#context-tab` in the dialog disclosure session stores selection; reopening starts on Settings, while undo/redo remounts preserve the user's tab. Advanced states are separate for Settings, Context and nested object bodies. Node id/label stay outside the config tabs; connections are authored on the canvas (ADR-0076). The primitive decision is [ADR-0031](decisions/ADR-0031-mounted-context-tabs.md).
 - **The disclosure.** `components/ui/disclosure.tsx`: a button with `aria-expanded` and `aria-controls`, a chevron that turns with the state, and the shared focus ring; Enter and Space toggle it. It starts collapsed. The node editor keeps which of its config form's disclosures are open (the Advanced group, collapsible list items, the cron schedule's raw expression) in its own state, which it passes to `SchemaForm` as `disclosures` (`forms/disclosures.ts`, keyed `#advanced`, by a stable row identity for collection items, and `<field>#advanced`): the remount an undo, a redo, or a subloop pick causes keeps them as the user left them, and closing the dialog or opening another node starts collapsed again. Without `disclosures` a form keeps them itself for as long as it is mounted. Its panel stays mounted while hidden, so its fields keep their state and validation. Each group is a section `Fieldset` with a `Legend` (borderless, a quiet uppercase heading, ruled off from the section before it). The loop panel's forms have no advanced fields and no disclosure.
 - **What a collapsed group hides.** The toggle's text, and so its accessible name, adds how many advanced fields hold a value of their own ("2 set": set and not their default; an object counts when any field inside does, so `{}` or a copy of each default does not, while an opaque JSON value such as a JSON Schema counts once supplied, `{}` included) and how many problems are inside ("1 error" on the bad tone with its glyph): schema issues, unparsed JSON text, and the loop's errors at or under an advanced field. The loop's errors are those its validation lists on the node with a field path, such as a Liquid template or JSONata expression that does not parse (the domain's syntax issues carry their field's path) and the API's checks; the node editor passes their paths to its config form (`SchemaForm`'s `problems`). Each path counts once. It shows while open too.
